#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 -  3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and $\\lambda =  -1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d353bf23-fca7-52bf-a7f3-afd4ed20c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077733} {:answer/id #uuid "d487b092-ee9a-57c6-b9b0-84830dba4996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077734} {:answer/id #uuid "2c18d718-4433-5823-a1ec-47e3df9987d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077735} {:answer/id #uuid "383dd0ef-57a7-596a-b378-20cd37ec74ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.