 "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} {:answer/id #uuid "7577a907-a24b-5a1a-9252-dbbbb82ae05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355696} {:answer/id #uuid "90cf1316-9d50-5a6d-896f-516e72559dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-14x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355942} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ddbcdd-67e2-59df-87ae-7afbcd997f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356128} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bcbb48-4d50-5e65-a98a-b2d2871a3b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f538e8-ffd5-580a-b787-3529e176d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c598865-38e3-50dc-b0e0-62772d08c731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5c18e84-2e36-57eb-8c9a-1a101f9f859c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90722" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-11\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+28y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-11\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+28{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43359a46-a31b-5979-b00d-e832c7aab359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421910} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b35fd-08b6-534e-aef6-d64720abed6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421915} {:answer/id #uuid "8b32650e-a838-5e42-b674-904b88326a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421917} {:answer/id #uuid "b72954b9-0f27-5ed9-92fd-03be2ce3c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421920} {:answer/id #uuid "c1df491c-fb96-5d15-b8cb-b70016700d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1df491c-fb96-5d15-b8cb-b70016700d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921} :answer-field/id #uuid "390cf455-f96c-59d6-8b71-3d6a053b53ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aecf7af1-6026-52e6-a339-611dfc1550ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90715" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+12{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\nI. $-3e^{-6x}$\n\nII. $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\nIII. $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-4.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a0b1d01-fdb0-586f-851e-92f612fa8195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077739} {:answer/id #uuid "99c73e0d-2724-51f3-bd81-32248af71bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077740} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d3275-316d-5442-80dd-540cedb8a306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077741} {:answer/id #uuid "b5df64b7-477d-5af9-8cf8-067d22153384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{6x} - 2e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077742} {:answer/id #uuid "cc314592-0237-5d57-837e-d318c8f94321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{6x} + e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a0b1d01-fdb0-586f-851e-92f612fa8195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077739} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ad0bcb-b98c-5662-9e8b-8b209506e628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077738}] :question/id #uuid "93a3e693-dee3-519b-b3b7-c74034cf9ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64659" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y^{″} - 5y^{′} - 6y = 0$, which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda-6=(\\lambda-6)(\\lambda+1).\n$$\n\nThus all solutions are linear combinations of $e^{6x}$ and $e^{-x}$. The option $e^{5x}$ is not such a solution: substitution gives\n\n$$\n(25-25-6)e^{5x}=-6e^{5x}\\ne0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221e1e12-fd82-5746-bf0f-8371e73f431f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333295} {:answer/id #uuid "468f21aa-b994-5cfb-9b54-4f931e72bcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333642} {:answer/id #uuid "702263ab-234b-55d2-9849-84e6a43e97f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334011} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b50263-23ea-593c-b199-69d4a7a58e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334540} {:answer/id #uuid "ef772abb-ba2a-5c3e-937a-89bc6a8a3afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221e1e12-fd82-5746-bf0f-8371e73f431f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333295} :answer-field/id #uuid "489185f8-eaf8-58ca-a92d-5b178a2e959a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f5e7eb7-af07-5752-88d0-e6c5860716ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64707" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-4t}$ and ${e}^{t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}$ is not a solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6b0f30-722e-564a-bc0f-567caceb21da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263530} {:answer/id #uuid "5c82e1c9-7216-598e-bed5-f25629a73c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263799} {:answer/id #uuid "6397e78e-daa7-5177-ba18-9151594d411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263861} {:answer/id #uuid "d767c68c-5d55-5b4e-b28e-a165a6cb90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264878} {:answer/id #uuid "e09f071c-e6a0-5382-aa02-707969b3720a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c82e1c9-7216-598e-bed5-f25629a73c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263799} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ada274-ae51-593f-9c0d-7d5e8a1a2519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c7e5301-fce4-581f-9a1b-7a5fdeeaa7ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-130123" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{3x}$ is not a solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ad620b-3fc3-5457-b0e3-5365f9109304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263245} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9e0533-11b3-5bc5-a88a-67a4e6340865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264231} {:answer/id #uuid "9730928e-aad3-5e78-9c5e-7d9033980232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264295} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b2ef53-8585-5367-ae62-ca4ee6637dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264597} {:answer/id #uuid "f2632b6d-569b-5600-aedc-7ef3ddd7e627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b2ef53-8585-5367-ae62-ca4ee6637dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264597} :answer-field/id #uuid "2397aa76-173b-51c5-9008-6a28b68229dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e70795d1-4d4c-517f-8763-1daa4c701147" :question/math-academy-id "q-130124" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}$ is not a solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dafe268-bb8a-597a-bc86-bb319556af55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333568} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb03da1-870f-5e36-9071-60c94ef4942b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333826} {:answer/id #uuid "93c51a10-13c5-5dc3-affa-5c2e7e487d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334304} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae8f7a-ee6d-5859-b1d3-3313fac24cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334485} {:answer/id #uuid "e9662fca-8c99-5f7b-850c-9aa6cdbae508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592